1. Security governance
Meridian will maintain a documented security programme proportionate to the service, including asset management,
access control, vulnerability management, secure development, logging, backup and recovery, vendor review and staff
awareness.
2. Identity and account controls
Business onboarding may include company-registry checks, authorised-representative verification, role-based access
and multi-factor authentication. A company’s legal existence does not by itself prove the authority or trustworthiness of
every account user.
3. Security incidents
A security incident includes suspected unauthorised access, credential compromise, material service disruption, data
exposure, malicious code or compromised integrations. Meridian will triage, contain, investigate, preserve relevant logs,
remediate and communicate as required by law and contract.
4. Customer cooperation
Customers must promptly report suspected compromise, rotate affected credentials, preserve relevant evidence and
cooperate with reasonable investigation steps.
5. Retention schedule
Proposed default periods, subject to legal and customer requirements: account profile data — account term plus [24]
months; transaction records and acceptance evidence — [7] years or the legally required period; telemetry and
supporting evidence — [24] months after transaction closure; security logs — [12] months; support tickets — [24]
months. Confirm periods with counsel and operational needs before launch.
6. Deletion and legal holds
Deletion may be delayed where information is subject to a legal hold, dispute, investigation, regulatory duty or secure
backup cycle. Deleted information will be removed from active systems within [30] days and from backups within [90]
days where technically feasible, unless law requires otherwise.
7. Data minimisation
Collect only information needed for the stated workflow. Avoid storing identity documents, precise location, biometric
data or sensitive personal data unless necessary, lawful and supported by appropriate safeguards.
8. Review
This schedule must be reviewed at least annually and after a material incident, major product change or significant legal
change.
